/**
 * Native latency measurement, warmup, and percentile reports.
 *
 * <p>{@link Bench} excludes optional warmup iterations and reports the count,
 * average, minimum, maximum, and six percentiles. {@link NanoBench} reports only
 * the count, average, minimum, and maximum in nanoseconds, without warmup or
 * percentiles. Both compile into the native executable.
 *
 * <p>Time an operation with {@link System#nanoTime()} and pass the elapsed
 * nanoseconds to {@link Bench#measure(long)} or {@link NanoBench#measure(long)}.
 * Alternatively, let the benchmark handle the clock: call {@link Bench#mark()}
 * or {@link NanoBench#mark()} before the operation, then {@link Bench#measure()}
 * or {@link NanoBench#measure()} afterward. The {@link Bench} and
 * {@link NanoBench} examples show both timing styles; setup, iteration counts,
 * reporting, and cleanup stay the same. Run warmup plus measurement iterations
 * when using Bench, and keep progress logging off while collecting timings.
 * Actual timings vary by machine.
 *
 * <p>Reset retains storage for reuse. Free each benchmark after use and free
 * any report String returned by {@code results()}. The class pages contain
 * complete examples and the timing, reset, and reporting contracts.
 *
 * @see Bench
 * @see NanoBench
 */
package ironwood.bench;
